 %=========================== [Abstract] ===============================
 \section*{Abstract}
+\phantomsection
 \addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
 In multinational organisations, meetings combine background noise, language switching, and domain-specific vocabulary, conditions under which general-purpose automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems degrade substantially. For employees with hearing loss, a reliable transcript can be an essential channel of access to meeting content. This thesis presents a controlled evaluation of a \textit{faster-whisper} pipeline, first studied on CPU and subsequently extended on GPU. A trilingual set of 36 MInDS-14 samples was constructed from 24 clean telephone-speech clips and 12 paired noise-augmented variants. In total, 468 transcription runs cover a pre-specified 180-run CPU campaign, a Whisper model-size ladder, VAD, a paired LoRA study, and wav2vec~2.0/MMS and Conformer baselines. On the 12 matched pairs, adding white noise at 8\,dB SNR raises mean WER from 61.1\% to 96.4\%, a paired penalty of 35.3 points (95\% bootstrap CI: [7.0, 60.0]). Spectral-gating noise reduction recovers 8.3 points on noisy variants while slightly degrading the full clean set. Forcing the language repairs several wrong-script outputs at no measurable computational cost. Model size remains the dominant accuracy lever: WER falls from 63.4\% with \textit{tiny} to 24.6\% with \textit{large-v3}. Prompting and LoRA each shift WER by only about one point in this domain, and the paired LoRA effect is not statistically established. In the historical 22-observation live study, excluding first utterances yields a median server-side post-endpointing latency of 0.56\,s and a p95 of 0.84\,s. The extended-log audit at the end of this thesis reports additional slower observations; these historical values are not an end-to-end guarantee. The study's scope is deliberately bounded: MInDS-14 is neither multi-speaker meeting audio nor Nestlé-domain speech, the diarization experiment validates plumbing rather than accuracy, and end-user accessibility is not evaluated within Topic~2.
