 Two confounds must be stated, and they are the same discipline applied to MMS. First, the Conformer receives the language \emph{by construction} (one monolingual checkpoint per language), which is architecturally equivalent to forced language identification; part of its apparent margin over \texttt{whisper-small} auto-detection is therefore not a pure architecture effect. The cleaner comparison is Conformer versus MMS, both CTC and language-given. On the same 36 files, MMS minus Conformer equals 9.1 WER points, with a 95\,\% paired-bootstrap interval of [3.0, 16.3]; Conformer is therefore lower on this particular sample. This remains a checkpoint-and-training-data comparison, not a universal proof of Conformer superiority. Second, MInDS-14 is 8\,kHz telephony upsampled to the 16\,kHz these wideband-trained models expect, which cannot restore the high-frequency information absent from the original recording. The practical conclusion is conditional: CTC checkpoints are fast and competitive when language is known, while Whisper offers native multilingual detection, punctuation and timestamps.
 \subsubsection{External Validity: Clean Read Speech (FLEURS)}
 As a cross-corpus check, the pipeline was additionally run on a corpus of a different nature: FLEURS (Google, CC-BY-4.0), clean, wideband, read speech, twenty clips per language (English, French, German). This set lies \emph{outside} the canonical 468-run campaign and its figures are never merged into the MInDS-14 tables; it serves only to test whether the difficulty measured on MInDS-14 is intrinsic to the pipeline or to the input. On clean read speech, \texttt{large-v3} reaches a mean WER of 7.4\,\% (median 3.4\,\%), against 24.6\,\% on MInDS-14, which indicates that the MInDS-14 difficulty stems from its 8\,kHz telephony, added noise and spontaneous speech rather than from a pipeline defect. One honest caveat bounds this check: on the longer FLEURS utterances the smaller checkpoints (\texttt{tiny} to \texttt{medium}) frequently enter a repetition loop (for example a twelve-word reference decoded as the token \enquote{und} repeated several hundred times), a documented long-form failure mode of Whisper's autoregressive decoder that its official decoding heuristics are designed to suppress \cite{radford2023robust,koenecke2024careless}. Because the evaluation harness keeps a plain deterministic decoding path identical to the MInDS-14 campaign for comparability, those loops are not suppressed; the FLEURS results for \texttt{tiny} to \texttt{medium} are therefore treated as measurements of that failure mode, not of transcription quality, and are excluded, with rare residual loops observed even at \texttt{large-v3}.
